#+title: {{ project_name }}

{{ description }}

A C++17 GPU program built with CMake and managed with mise.  It includes the
PhysX umbrella header, prints the SDK version it was compiled against, and
creates and releases a =PxFoundation=, a =PxPhysics=, and a CUDA context
manager before exiting.

* Requirements

- An installed PhysX 5 SDK (headers plus the =bin/linux.x86_64/<config>/=
  libraries, including =libPhysXGpu_64.so=)
- An NVIDIA GPU with a working driver.  The CUDA toolkit is /not/ required --
  nothing CUDA is linked, and the driver is reached at run time.
- CMake 3.16 or newer and a C++17 compiler
- mise

* Build and check

#+begin_src shell
mise build    # configure + compile -> build/{{ project_slug }}
mise check    # build, then run it as a smoke test
#+end_src

The program exits non-zero if =PxCreateFoundation=, =PxCreatePhysics=, or
=PxCreateCudaContextManager= fails, so =mise check= is a real test of the link
and of the GPU backend, not just of the compile.  There is no separate task for
running the binary -- =./build/{{ project_slug }}= does that, and a task named
=run= would collide with mise's own =mise run= subcommand.

If you drive CMake directly instead of mise, the bundled =CMakePresets.json=
reproduces the same configuration (build directory =build/=, Release build
type, release PhysX libraries) without typing the flags:

#+begin_src shell
cmake --list-presets            # list available presets
cmake --preset default          # configure
cmake --build --preset default  # compile -> build/{{ project_slug }}
#+end_src

Presets require CMake 3.21 or newer; the plain =cmake -S . -B build=
commands in =mise.toml= work from CMake 3.16.  Both entry points write to the
same =build/= directory.

* Choosing the PhysX installation

PhysX installs no CMake config package, so =CMakeLists.txt= finds the SDK by
hand.  It treats as the SDK root whichever directory contains
=include/PxPhysicsAPI.h=, checking in order:

1. =-DPHYSX_ROOT=/path/to/physx= (a CMake cache variable)
2. =$PHYSX_ROOT= in the environment
3. =$HOME/local=
4. =/usr/local=

If none matches, configure fails with a message naming =-DPHYSX_ROOT=, rather
than failing later in the compiler with a missing-header error.

=PHYSX_BUILD_TYPE= picks which installed library directory is linked:

#+begin_src shell
cmake -S . -B build -DPHYSX_BUILD_TYPE=checked
#+end_src

It accepts =debug=, =checked=, =profile=, or =release= (the default), and has
to name a configuration that was actually built: the directories are
=bin/linux.x86_64/<config>/=, so an SDK installed only in release has no
=checked= libraries to find.  Configure reports the resolved paths:

#+begin_src text
-- PhysX root : /home/you/local
-- PhysX libs : /home/you/local/bin/linux.x86_64/release (release)
#+end_src

=PHYSX_BUILD_TYPE= also chooses the default =CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE= for this
project's own sources -- =Debug= for =debug= and =checked=, =Release=
otherwise -- but the two are independent, and =-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE= overrides
it.

* Walkthrough of =src/main.cpp=

The file is short on purpose, and each part is load-bearing:

- =#include <PxPhysicsAPI.h>= is the SDK's umbrella header; including it is
  itself a check that the include path is right.
- =PX_PHYSICS_VERSION_MAJOR= and friends come from
  =foundation/PxPhysicsVersion.h= and describe the /headers/.
- =PxDefaultAllocator= and =PxDefaultErrorCallback= (from =extensions/=) are
  required because PhysX reports errors through callbacks rather than by
  throwing exceptions.
- =PxCreateFoundation= and =PxCreatePhysics= are declared in the /global/
  namespace, while the types they take and return (=physx::PxFoundation=,
  =physx::PxPhysics=, =physx::PxTolerancesScale=) live in =physx::=.  Mixing
  up the two is the most common first compile error in PhysX code.
- =#include <gpu/PxGpu.h>= pulls in =cudamanager/PxCudaContextManager.h= and
  declares =PxCreateCudaContextManager=.  It needs no CUDA headers at all:
  PhysX keeps the driver types opaque precisely so that an SDK consumer does
  not need the toolkit installed.
- All three factory functions return null rather than aborting -- on version
  mismatch for the first two, and on a backend that will not load for the
  third -- which is why each return value is checked.
- Objects are released in reverse order of creation; PhysX has no garbage
  collection and no exceptions to unwind with.

The program prints the header version, not the runtime version: PhysX 5 exposes
no public API for the latter, and =PxCreateFoundation= returning non-null is
the SDK's own assertion that the two agree.

* How the GPU backend is reached

Nothing CUDA is linked, which is the part that surprises people.  A program can
name only the five PhysX static libraries and still run on the GPU:

- =libPhysXGpu_64.so=, the GPU backend, is loaded by the SDK with =dlopen= at
  run time.  It appears neither in the link line nor in =ldd= output, so a
  program can link perfectly and still fail at the first
  =PxCreateCudaContextManager= with a null return.
- The NVIDIA driver is reached the same way.  No PhysX library, static or
  shared, lists =libcuda= in its =NEEDED= entries, and no static library
  references a =cu*= symbol -- confirm with =readelf -d= and =nm -u=.  Adding
  =-lcuda= by hand compiles and runs, but the linker drops it as unused.
- So the CUDA toolkit is needed neither at build time nor at run time.  Only
  the driver is, and it has to be present when the program /runs/, not when it
  links.

The build's one real obligation is making that =dlopen= succeed.
=CMakeLists.txt= gives the executable a =BUILD_RPATH=/=INSTALL_RPATH= of the
PhysX library directory, so no =LD_LIBRARY_PATH= is needed.  To confirm the
rpath is doing the work rather than an inherited environment variable, run the
binary with the search path cleared:

#+begin_src shell
env -u LD_LIBRARY_PATH ./build/{{ project_slug }}
#+end_src

